/F1 18 Tf
(JUDGE Q&A | PART 3) Tj
T*
T*
/F1 12 Tf
(Q17. Why not translate whole sentences?) Tj
T*
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: This prototype predicts limited labels. Sentence-level sign-language translation needs) Tj
T*
(richer data and models for grammar, context, movement and other visual cues.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q18. Can it replace a human interpreter?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: No. It is a limited assistive prototype and should not be relied on for high-stakes) Tj
T*
(conversations.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q19. Difference between recognition and translation?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: Recognition predicts a sign label. Translation converts text or a recognized label into) Tj
T*
(another language; that does not mean the system understood a full signed sentence.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q20. What will you improve next?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: Expand and balance the dataset, test across signers and lighting, publish honest) Tj
T*
(metrics, improve sequence recognition, and conduct usability testing with the intended) Tj
T*
(community.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q21. What if the prediction is wrong?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: The user should be able to repeat or correct the message. Predictions should not be) Tj
T*
(treated as certain, especially in important conversations.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q22. What makes this scientific?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: It has a defined problem, technical method, testable outputs, limitations, and a plan) Tj
T*
(to evaluate performance rather than relying on a visual demo alone.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q23. Why is diverse data important?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: Hands, signing styles, speed, camera angles, and backgrounds vary. A model trained on a) Tj
T*
(few people may fail for others.) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Q24. Main conclusion?) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(A: Computer vision can support a limited sign-recognition prototype, but meaningful) Tj
T*
(accessibility requires accuracy testing, user involvement, privacy, and respect for) Tj
T*
(sign-language complexity.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
